<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Australas. Chiropr. Osteopathy</journal-id><journal-title>Australasian Chiropractic &#x00026; Osteopathy</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">1328-0384</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">17987199</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2051301</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>RISK MANAGEMENT FOR CHIROPRACTORS AND OSTEOPATHS</article-title><subtitle>Neck Manipulation &#x00026; Vertebrobasilar Stroke</subtitle></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Reggars</surname><given-names>John W.</given-names></name><degrees>D.C., M.Chiro.Sc.</degrees></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>French</surname><given-names>Simon D.</given-names></name><degrees>B.App.Sc.(Chiro.), M.P.H.</degrees></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Walker</surname><given-names>Bruce F.</given-names></name><degrees>D.C., M.P.H.</degrees></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Cameron</surname><given-names>Melainie</given-names></name><degrees>B.App.Sc.(Osteo.), M.H.Sc.</degrees></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Pollard</surname><given-names>Henry</given-names></name><degrees>Ph.D., B.Sc., Grad.Dip.(Chiro.), Grad.Dip.App.Sc., M.Sport.Sc.</degrees></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Vitiello</surname><given-names>Andrew</given-names></name><degrees>B.Sc., M.Chiro.</degrees></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Werth</surname><given-names>Peter D.</given-names></name><degrees>B.App.Sc.(Chiro.)</degrees></contrib></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>3</month><year>2003</year></pub-date><volume>11</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>9</fpage><lpage>15</lpage><copyright-statement>Chiropractic &#x00026; Osteopathic College of Australasia</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2003</copyright-year><abstract><p>Although rare, vertebrobasilar stroke is the best known of the possible side effects of cervical manipulation. Due to the serious sequelae that may result from cervical manipulation, chiropractors and osteopaths must take the appropriate steps to ensure the risk is minimised. This article outlines how the astute practitioner can minimise this risk. Practitioners must decide on the options for treatment of a patient with neck problems. Practitioners must also advise the patient of these options as part of an appropriate informed consent.	  </p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>Chiropractic</kwd><kwd>stroke</kwd><kwd>manipulation</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>